# Stop building a master persistent graph; model identity as an ephemeral consensus engine
def resolve_edge(event, deterministic_tokens, ambient_signals):
    # 1. Hard fail on consent first; dynamic state invalidates static cached mappings
    consent = evaluate_revocable_token(event.consent_chain, event.jurisdiction)
    if not consent.permits(event.purpose): return EphemeralAnonymousContext()

    # 2. Prefer clean-room deterministic joins, but enforce an aggressive decay shelf-life
    direct_match = query_federated_mesh(deterministic_tokens, max_staleness_hours=72)
    if direct_match and direct_match.confidence >= 0.99:
        return ScopedCluster(direct_match.id, ttl=consent.duration_limit)

    # 3. Reject fuzzy IPs; use real-time graph consensus bounded by acceptable error budget
    # Probabilistic linkage only survives if both parties can mathematically bound false-positive risk
    prob_cluster = derive_consensus(ambient_signals, error_threshold=event.max_fp_risk)
    if prob_cluster.entropy_score < consent.leakage_budget:
        return prob_cluster.as_scoped_view()

    # 4. Fail closed: treat as novel endpoint to avoid polluting downstream measurement
    return SyntheticPartitionId(event.session_context)

One graph will not come —
cap loosely, suppress with proof;
consent rides each edge.

Hush now, don’t forge one ID to follow everywhere;
Start with consent receipts and purpose rules each channel has to share.

Link only deterministic signals, scoped and held apart;
Let uncertain matches stay uncertain, not masquerade as smart.

Test suppression and frequency caps for drift, mistakes, and harm;
Prefer a missed connection to a false, privacy-breaking charm.

Clean rooms may measure overlap, not make lost truth appear;
We’ll learn where recognition fails—the answer won’t be settled here.
